course: dictionary
lesson: 3130
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context shows which one the writer means. The dictionary gives elephant two meanings: a very large grey animal, and a saying about a big problem that everyone knows about but nobody talks about.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «An African elephant's two huge ears» is about the animal, because ears can flap and cool it down. When a sentence is about a problem that nobody mentions, the saying is the meaning to choose. Ask yourself: does this meaning fit the whole sentence?
explain: Check the facts too. A sentence like «Elephants are not terrified of mice» tests you: a good scientist checks a story before repeating it. Read carefully, match the meaning to the clues, and check what you read.
fact: An elephant is a very large grey animal with a long trunk, big ears and usually tusks.
fact: Elephants live in Africa and Asia. They are mammals and herbivores, which means they eat plants.
fact: The African elephant is the largest land animal, and an adult elephant can weigh about 6 tonnes.
fact: «The elephant in the room» means a big problem everyone knows about but nobody talks about.
fact: A «white elephant» is a costly, useless thing.
exercise: «An African elephant has two finger-like tips at the end of its trunk.» What is a trunk? | A large box for storing toys | A tusk | *The long nose of an elephant | A type of tree
exercise: Where do elephants live? | In Europe only | In the sea | *In Africa and Asia | In Australia only
exercise: A maths problem says an adult elephant can weigh about 6 tonnes. Is that heavy or light? | *Very heavy | Very light, like a feather | Just right for a cat | Exactly the same as a mouse
test: Which phrase means a problem everyone knows about but nobody talks about? | A white elephant | A herbivore | *The elephant in the room | An elephant never forgets
test: «Elephants are not terrified of mice.» What does this sentence ask you to do? | Forget everything you read | Believe every story you hear | Be scared of mice | *Check a story before you repeat it
exam: «An elephant never forgets.» Is this a real scientific fact or a saying? | A word from a maths problem | A fact about every elephant's memory | A dictionary definition of elephant | *A saying, not a scientific fact
